<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roquebrune-Cap-Martin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roquebrune-Cap-Martin is a commune on the French Riviera between Monaco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a>, centred on a hilltop village below a medieval castle. Long part of the Principality of Monaco, it joined Menton in breaking away in 1848 and was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1861. The architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a> spent his summers there and is buried in its cemetery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roquebrune-cap-martin.jpg" alt="Roquebrune-Cap-Martin" width="320" height="126" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: XtoF · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RocquebruneCapMarrin_(pixinn.net).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
